# The waypoints kept for the 26B A4B, whose feed forward is a mixture.
#
# Every one of its thirty blocks is a mixture block, so the choice of blocks is
# about the attention rather than the experts: 0 and 1 are window blocks, 5 is
# the first global one — sixteen query heads of five hundred and twelve over
# two key-value heads, a geometry no other checkpoint here has — and 6 and 17
# are two more, far apart, because a router that is right at block 0 and wrong
# at block 17 is a stride bug rather than an arithmetic one.
prompt      The capital of France is
add_special 1

blocks 0 1 5 6 17

global inp_scaled
global result_norm

require attn_norm
require Qcur_normed
require Qcur_pos
require kqv_out
require attn_post_norm
require attn_out
require l_out

# The mixture's own nodes, named by models/gemma4.cpp. ffn_moe_logits is the
# one that matters most: a wrong choice of experts produces fluent text, so a
# test comparing only l_out would pass a long way past the bug.
require ffn_norm_1
require ffn_norm_2
require ffn_moe_logits
require ffn_mlp
require ffn_moe
require ffn_moe_combined
require ffn_post_norm

# A global block computes no value projection of its own: its keys are its
# values. So these are asked for and allowed to be absent.
optional Kcur_normed
optional Kcur_pos
optional Vcur_normed

# Every block's output, so that a divergence in the full stack says which block
# it began in.
all_blocks l_out
